<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Copyright (C) 2026 SharpEmu Emulator Project
SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later

Hardened-runtime entitlements for SharpEmu.app, applied by
scripts/macos/build-app.sh when it signs with --options runtime.

This file is the *source*, not what codesign receives: codesign's entitlements
parser (AMFIUnserializeXML) rejects XML comments, so build-app.sh runs the file
through `plutil -convert xml1` first, which strips them.

Only the main executable needs these. Nested dylibs and frameworks inherit the
host executable's entitlements, so the script signs them without an
entitlements file and applies this one to the bundle itself.

Each entry is a capability the hardened runtime otherwise denies. They are
listed with their justification because every one of them weakens a platform
defence, and the set should shrink as the emulator changes — in particular, a
Native AOT build would need none of them.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      Required. CoreCLR compiles managed IL to machine code at runtime and
      needs MAP_JIT pages to do it. Microsoft documents this entitlement as
      required for any .NET app that is not published as Native AOT. Without
      it the runtime cannot allocate executable memory and the process dies
      before Main.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Required by the emulator specifically, beyond ordinary .NET needs.
      SharpEmu executes guest PS5 x86-64 code: it maps the guest address space
      and makes guest-supplied pages executable, which is writable-then-
      executable memory that did not come from MAP_JIT and carries no
      signature. This is also the entitlement the `dotnet` host itself is
      signed with. It is the broadest permission here; it is required because
      "run code the user supplied" is what an emulator is.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      Required while the bundle is ad-hoc signed and while it carries
      third-party natives. Library validation only admits dylibs signed by
      Apple or by the same Team ID as the host, and an ad-hoc signature has no
      Team ID at all. The bundle loads FFmpeg dylibs from sharpemu/ffmpeg-core
      plus Skia/HarfBuzz/SDL natives from NuGet packages. Re-signing all of
      them with one Developer ID identity would remove the need for this
      entitlement; revisit it when a real signing identity is in use.

Deliberately absent:

  com.apple.security.get-task-allow and com.apple.security.cs.debugger
      These let another process inspect or debug this one. Useful for a local
      debug build, wrong for a distributed artifact, and get-task-allow makes
      notarization fail. Add them ad hoc when debugging a packaged build.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      SharpEmu does not need DYLD_* to find its own libraries; everything
      resolves from the bundle via AppContext.BaseDirectory. Granting it would
      let an attacker inject a library through the environment.

  App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox)
      Not enabled. The emulator reads game dumps from arbitrary user-chosen
      paths; a sandboxed build would need a document-scoped security model that
      does not exist yet.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
